nce.\n9: \n10:     Input structure:\n11:     - bg = background. Border color forms equal-sized framed squares with unique inner fill colors.\n12:     - Control row below squares: colored pixels read left-to-right encode visitation order.\n13: \n14:     Rule:\n15:     - Start at the square matching first control color c0.\n16:     - For each consecutive pair (ci, ci+1), find the ci+1-colored square aligned in the same row or column band, preferring nearest.\n17:     - Draw a bridge colored ci between the pair, spanning the gap with inner-square thickness.\n18: \n19:     Key insight: Row/column-band alignment plus proximity resolves ambiguity when inner colors repeat.\n20:     \"\"\"\n21:     g = np.array(grid, dtype=int)\n22:     H, W = g.shape\n23:     bg = int(get_bg(g))\n24: \n25:     # border color = most common non-bg color\n26:     cnt = Counter(map(int, g.flatten()))\n27:     border = None\n28:     for col, _k in cnt.most_common():\n29:         if int(col) != int(bg):\n30:             border = int(col)\n31:             break\n32:     assert not (border is None)\n33: \n34:     # detect framed squares as connected components of the border color\n35:     lab, n = label_mask(g == int(border), connectivity=4)\n36:     frames: list[dict] = []\n37:     for cid in range(1, int(n) + 1):\n38:         pts = np.argwhere(lab == int(cid))\n39:         if int(pts.shape[0]) < 8:\n40:             continue\n41:         r0, c0 = pts.min(axis=0)\n42:         r1, c1 = pts.max(axis=0)\n43:         r0, c0, r1, c1 = map(int, (r0, c0, r1, c1))\n44:         h = int(r1 - r0 + 1)\n45:         w = int(c1 - c0 + 1)\n46:         if h < 3 or w < 3:\n47:             continue\n48: \n49:         t = None\n50:         inner_col = None\n51:         for tt in range(1, int(min(h, w) // 2) + 1):\n52:             inner = g[int(r0 + tt) : int(r1 - tt) + 1, int(c0 + tt) : int(c1 - tt) + 1]\n53:             if inner.size == 0:\n54:                 continue\n55:             if bool((inner == int(border)).any()):\n56:                 continue\n57:             u = set(map(int, inner.flatten()))\n58:             if len(u) != 1:\n59:                 continue\n60: \n61:             ok = True\n62:             if not bool((g[int(r0) : int(r0 + tt), int(c0) : int(c1) + 1] == int(border)).all()):\n63:                 ok = False\n64:             if not bool((g[int(r1 - tt) + 1 : int(r1) + 1, int(c0) : int(c1) + 1] == int(border)).all()):\n65:                 ok = False\n66:             if not bool((g[int(r0) : int(r1) + 1, int(c0) : int(c0 + tt)] == int(border)).all()):\n67:                 ok = False\n68:             if not bool((g[int(r0) : int(r1) + 1, int(c1 - tt) + 1 : int(c1) + 1] == int(border)).all()):\n69:                 ok = False\n70:             if not ok:\n71:                 continue\n72: \n73:             t = int(tt)\n74:             inner_col = int(next(iter(u)))\n75:             break\n76: \n77:         if t is None or inner_col is None:\n78:             continue\n79: \n80:         frames.append(\n81:             {\n82:                 \"bbox\": (int(r0), int(c0), int(r1), int(c1)),\n83:                 \"t\": int(t),\n84:                 \"inner\": int(inner_col),\n85:                 \"inner_bbox\": (int(r0 + t), int(c0 + t), int(r1 - t), int(c1 - t)),\n86:             }\n87:         )\n88: \n89:     assert not (not frames)\n90: \n91:     by: dict[int, list[dict]] = defaultdict(list)\n92:     for f in frames:\n93:         by[int(f[\"inner\"])].append(f)\n94: \n95:     max_frame_r = max(int(f[\"bbox\"][2]) for f in frames)\n\n425: \n426: \n427: def generate_input() -> np.ndarray:\n428:     for _ in range(500):\n429:         result = _gen_attempt()\n430:         if result is not None:\n431:             return result\n432:     raise RuntimeError(\"Failed to generate valid input after 500 attempts\")\n\n===== /home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/89565ca0.py\n117: \n118: \n119: def main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray:\n120:     \"\"\"\n121:     Convert each colored partitioned frame into a horizontal bar whose length is its number of enclosed regions.\n122: \n123:     Input structure:\n124:     - Black background with several axis-aligned colored line drawings.\n125:     - The line drawings are damaged by scattered pixels of one non-background noise color and by overlaps with other drawings.\n126:     - Each real drawing is a rectangular partitioned frame enclosing one or more background regions.\n127: \n128:     Rule:\n129:     - For each non-background color, reconstruct its horizontal and vertical wall segments from dense row/column evidence.\n130:     - Count the 4-connected background regions fully enclosed by those reconstructed walls.\n131:     - The color with no enclosed region is the noise/pad color.\n132:     - Sort drawing colors by enclosed-region count; equal counts are ordered by which reconstructed frame covers the other.\n133: \n134:     Key insight:\n135:     - The scattered color is not an object; it only pads the histogram, while each true object's room count is stable after rectilinear wall reconstruction.\n136:     \"\"\"\n137:     g = np.asarray(grid, dtype=int)\n138:     bg = int(get_bg(g))\n139:     assert bg == 0\n140: \n141:     counts: list[tuple[int, int, np.ndarray]] = []\n142:     for color in colors_present(g, exclude={bg}):\n143:         rooms = _room_count_for_color(g, int(color))\n144:         walls = _walls_for_color(g, int(color))\n145:         counts.append((int(color), int(rooms), walls))\n146: \n147:     drawings = [(color, rooms, walls) for color, rooms, walls in counts if rooms > 0]\n148:     pad_colors = [color for color, rooms, _walls in counts if rooms == 0]\n149:     assert drawings\n150:     assert len(pad_colors) == 1\n151: \n152:     pad = int(pad_colors[0])\n153:     ordered: list[tuple[int, int, np.ndarray]] = []\n154:     for rooms in sorted({rooms for _color, rooms, _walls in drawings}):\n155:         group = [item for item in drawings if item[1] == rooms]\n156:      